  -- ── issue mappings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- pack-generation: derived — the fingerprint moves with these rows; every
  --   affected member's pack retires and rebuilds on the next read.
  --
  -- Every row is is_primary = true, and that is a rule 30 decision rather than a
  -- convenience. Rule 30 says to check the primary wall BEFORE shipping a depth pass:
  -- a member who crosses four acts on a key with no primary act stops reading as
  -- 'thin but one-sided' and starts reading as 'not about this issue'. Because every
  -- row here is primary, any member this wave carries to four acts on one of these
  -- four keys arrives holding a primary this wave supplied.

  -- H.R. 1047 · energy_production w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 3 (RULEMAKING TO IMPROVE INTERCONNECTION QUEUE FLEXIBILITY) gives the
  --   Commission ninety days to initiate a rulemaking that, in the Act's own
  --   words, ensures 'new dispatchable power projects that improve grid
  --   reliability and resource adequacy can interconnect to the electric grid
  --   quickly, cost-effectively, and reliably', and directs it to amend the pro
  --   forma Large Generator Interconnection Procedures promulgated under 18
  --   C.F.R. 35.28(f). The direction on the policy is the word dispatchable: the
  --   Act does not shorten every queue, it moves firm generating capacity to the
  --   front of one. energy_production's written scope in issue-scope.js names
  --   'how much conventional supply and firm generating capacity exists' as what
  --   is IN and names 'dispatchable-resource state policy' among its
  --   instruments, and its pole reads 'Advanced = the vote expanded conventional
  --   supply or kept generation online'. A yea did that.
  --
  --   DECLINED permitting_reform: This is the closest call in the wave and it is
  --   refused on the written scope rather than on feel. permitting_reform is
  --   'how long a federal review of a PROJECT takes and who may challenge it:
  --   the scope of environmental review, statutory page and deadline limits on
  --   it, lead-agency and one-federal-decision consolidation, and the window in
  --   which a completed permit may be sued over'. H.R. 1047 touches none of
  --   those four things. An interconnection queue is a Commission tariff
  --   procedure under 18 C.F.R. 35.28(f), not an environmental review; no page
  --   limit, no lead agency, no litigation window appears in the text. Filing it
  --   here would print a reader's vote on which generators connect as a vote on
  --   how long NEPA takes.
  --
  --   DECLINED climate_action: No emissions programme is created, funded,
  --   repealed or taxed. climate_action's pole is written entirely in those
  --   terms and the Act contains none of them.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3062 · permitting_reform w90 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 (STRENGTHENING NORTH AMERICAN ENERGY SECURITY) replaces the
  --   Presidential-permit practice for border-crossing facilities with a
  --   statutory certificate-of-crossing process carrying its own decision
  --   deadline and its own agency assignment. The Act's own long title states
  --   the axis — 'a more uniform, transparent, and modern process to authorize'
  --   — and process is what the text changes. That is permitting_reform's
  --   subject: the scope of a federal authorisation, who runs it and by when.
  --   Weighted 90 rather than 100 because the long title names a motive beyond
  --   the key, the import and export of oil and natural gas, and the weight is
  --   where that is recorded rather than in a second row.
  --
  --   DECLINED energy_production: Tempting, and refused, because the Act does
  --   not authorise a single facility: it changes who issues the authorisation
  --   and on what clock. It also covers 'the transmission of electricity'
  --   alongside oil and natural gas, so a row on a conventional-supply key would
  --   characterise a vote about a transmission line as a vote for conventional
  --   supply. The motive is carried in the weight above, not in a row that says
  --   something the text does not.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: Runbook rule 3: gov_regulation is the regulatory
  --   question, not every mandate. A statutory permit process for cross-border
  --   facilities is a permitting instrument.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 1366 · lands_energy w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 (HARDROCK MINING MILL SITES) amends section 42 of the Revised
  --   Statutes (30 U.S.C. 42) so that a claimant may locate and use mill sites
  --   for operations ancillary to a mining claim without the acreage and use
  --   constraints the current section imposes, and the Act establishes the
  --   Abandoned Hardrock Mine Fund alongside it. Both limbs are hardrock mining
  --   on the federal estate, which is the whole of lands_energy's published
  --   chip: 'Expand domestic energy, mining & resource development'. The entire
  --   subject of the measure is the key, so weight 100.
  --
  --   DECLINED lands_preserve: The mirror row is not written. lands_preserve is
  --   a distinct key with its own instruments and a D lean in ISSUE_MAP, and
  --   filing every mining bill against it would manufacture a conservation
  --   record out of a mining vote for every member who voted nay. A nay on this
  --   Act is already published as 'against' on lands_energy, which is the true
  --   sentence.
  --
  --   DECLINED permitting_reform: No environmental review, deadline, lead agency
  --   or litigation window is touched. The Act changes what a claimant may
  --   locate, not how long a review of it runs.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 4776 · permitting_reform w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   This is the key's own subject with nothing left over. SEC. 2 (NEPA REFORM)
  --   amends the National Environmental Policy Act itself; SEC. 3 (JUDICIAL
  --   REVIEW) sets the window and the standing conditions on which a completed
  --   environmental document may be challenged; SEC. 4 preserves ongoing
  --   administrative corrections. permitting_reform's written scope names
  --   exactly these limbs — 'the scope of environmental review, statutory page
  --   and deadline limits on it' and 'the window in which a completed permit may
  --   be sued over' — and its pole reads 'Advanced = narrower review and firm
  --   deadlines'. A yea narrowed the review and shortened the window to sue.
  --   Weight 100.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: Runbook rule 3. NEPA reform is the
  --   environmental-review question and it has its own key; gov_regulation is
  --   the size and posture of the federal rulebook. permitting_reform's own
  --   scope note names 'the size of the federal rulebook generally (Cut Federal
  --   Red Tape)' as OUT, which is the same wall read from the other side.
  --
  --   DECLINED climate_action: Narrowing environmental review is not repealing
  --   an emissions programme. climate_action's scope note names 'how long a
  --   project review takes' as OUT in terms.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3616 · energy_production w90 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 amends section 215 of the Federal Power Act (16 U.S.C. 824o) to
  --   build a chain of binding duties, not a study. Subsection (g)(2) requires
  --   the Electric Reliability Organization's assessment to include an annual
  --   long-term assessment ending in 'a determination of whether additional
  --   generation resources are necessary'; (g)(3) requires that, on such a
  --   finding, the ERO 'shall publicly notify the Commission that the bulk-power
  --   system is in a state of generation inadequacy'; and new subsection (h)
  --   then requires the Commission to notify the Department of Energy, the
  --   Environmental Protection Agency and any other agency it names, after which
  --   'the head of each Federal agency that received such notice shall provide
  --   to the Commission for review and comment any covered agency action' before
  --   that action reaches the Federal Register, and the Commission 'shall, by
  --   order' return comments. That is an operative interposition into other
  --   agencies' rulemakings, triggered by a generation-adequacy finding, and its
  --   object is keeping generation online — energy_production's pole in terms.
  --   Weight 90 because the long title names a motive beyond the key, the
  --   reliable operation of the bulk-power system.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: Refused under runbook rule 3, and this measure is
  --   the wave's clearest test of that rule because the Act's own long title
  --   says 'review regulations'. gov_regulation is the regulatory question — the
  --   size and posture of the federal rulebook as such — not every instrument
  --   that touches a rulemaking. Here the trigger is a generation-inadequacy
  --   determination, the object under review is a rule's effect on the
  --   bulk-power system, and the Act reaches no other rulemaking for any other
  --   reason. Filing it on gov_regulation would print a vote about grid
  --   reliability as a general deregulatory vote, and would hand every member
  --   holding it a fourth non-primary act on a key whose Senate primary problem
  --   runbook rule 30 already documents at length.
  --
  --   DECLINED checks_balances: The Act redistributes review duties among
  --   executive agencies. Congress and the courts gain nothing, so the key's
  --   subject is absent.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3632 · energy_production w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 amends section 207 of the Federal Power Act and requires an owner
  --   or operator that retires an electric generating unit to give notice of the
  --   planned retirement before it happens. energy_production's written scope in
  --   issue-scope.js names, as part of what is IN, 'the determinations required
  --   before an existing plant may close' — this measure is that clause and
  --   nothing else, which is why the weight is 100 and why the key needed no
  --   argument beyond quoting itself.
  --
  --   DECLINED climate_action: A retirement-notice duty is not an emissions
  --   programme in either direction. The Act neither requires nor forbids the
  --   retirement; it requires that it be announced.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3668 · permitting_reform w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 (PROMOTING INTERAGENCY COORDINATION FOR REVIEW OF NATURAL GAS
  --   PIPELINES) makes the Commission the lead agency for authorisations under
  --   section 3 of the Natural Gas Act and binds the other agencies whose
  --   sign-off a project needs to the Commission's schedule. permitting_reform's
  --   written scope names 'lead-agency and one-federal-decision consolidation'
  --   as what is IN, verbatim; this Act is that clause applied to one statute.
  --   Weight 100.
  --
  --   DECLINED energy_production: The Act authorises no pipeline and expands no
  --   acreage. It coordinates the agencies that review an application, which is
  --   the process, and the process key already carries it at full weight.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3898 · permitting_reform w80 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   Five of the Act's ten sections are permit-process sections under the Clean
  --   Water Act and they are what the Act is named for: SEC. 5 (IMPROVING WATER
  --   QUALITY CERTIFICATIONS AND AMERICAN ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE) narrows the
  --   section 401 certification a project must obtain from a State, which is the
  --   single most litigated project-review step in the statute; SEC. 6
  --   (CLARIFYING FEDERAL GENERAL PERMITS) widens what a general permit may
  --   cover in place of an individual review; SEC. 7 (NPDES PERMIT TERMS) sets
  --   the terms of a discharge permit; SEC. 8 (CONFIDENCE IN CLEAN WATER
  --   PERMITS) fixes the finality a permittee may rely on and therefore the
  --   ground on which a permit may be attacked; SEC. 10 exempts agricultural
  --   stormwater discharge. Those are the scope of a federal project review, the
  --   substitution of a general authorisation for an individual one, and the
  --   window in which a completed permit may be challenged — permitting_reform's
  --   written scope in three of its own clauses, with a pole of 'narrower review
  --   and firm deadlines' that a yea advanced.
  --   
  --   DISCLOSED PARTIAL COVERAGE, because half the Act is not on this key and
  --   pretending otherwise is what weight 100 would have said. SEC. 2 (WATER
  --   QUALITY STANDARDS ATTAINABILITY), SEC. 3 (WATER QUALITY CRITERIA
  --   DEVELOPMENT AND TRANSPARENCY) and SEC. 4 (WATER QUALITY TECHNOLOGY
  --   AVAILABILITY) change the substantive standard-setting rules of the Clean
  --   Water Act, and SEC. 9 concerns forest roads and wildland firefighter
  --   safety. No key on the live topic tree covers substantive water-quality
  --   standards: `water` is Water Conservation, whose written pole is 'Advanced
  --   = the vote reduced, priced or measured water use' and whose scope note
  --   excludes the supply and works side in terms, so loosening a pollution
  --   standard is not in it in either direction; `water_storage` is reservoirs
  --   and pipelines; `enviro_balance` is a `*_balance` key and is structurally
  --   suppressed from every read. Those four sections are therefore recorded
  --   here as uncovered and are deliberately not mapped. Weight 80 rather than
  --   100 is the honest arithmetic of that: the permit sections are the
  --   operative axis of a larger act.
  --
  --   DECLINED water: Cousin wall, and the seed says it out loud because the
  --   bill has the word water in nine of its ten section headings. `water` is
  --   Water Conservation — 'Advanced = the vote reduced, priced or measured
  --   water use'. This Act does not touch how much water anyone uses; it changes
  --   water-quality standards and discharge permits. A row here would print a
  --   vote about pollution limits as a vote about conservation, and its own
  --   scope note already warns that filing the wrong instrument here 'would
  --   print it as a conservation record'.
  --
  --   DECLINED water_storage: No reservoir, pipeline, recycling or storage
  --   capacity appears in the text.
  --
  --   DECLINED enviro_balance: A `*_balance` key. stance-helpers.js suppresses
  --   every read on a balance key with reason `balance_key`, so a row here would
  --   characterise nothing while appearing to cover the bill — which is the
  --   definition of parking a measure. The brief forbids it and so does the
  --   engine.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: Runbook rule 3.

DISCLOSED PARTIAL COVERAGE, because half the Act is not on this key and pretending otherwise is what weight 100 would have said. SEC. 2 (WATER QUALITY STANDARDS ATTAINABILITY), SEC. 3 (WATER QUALITY CRITERIA DEVELOPMENT AND TRANSPARENCY) and SEC. 4 (WATER QUALITY TECHNOLOGY AVAILABILITY) change the substantive standard-setting rules of the Clean Water Act, and SEC. 9 concerns forest roads and wildland firefighter safety. No key on the live topic tree covers substantive water-quality standards: `water` is Water Conservation, whose written pole is ''Advanced = the vote reduced, priced or measured water use'' and whose scope note excludes the supply and works side in terms, so loosening a pollution standard is not in it in either direction; `water_storage` is reservoirs and pipelines; `enviro_balance` is a `*_balance` key and is structurally suppressed from every read. Those four sections are therefore recorded here as uncovered and are deliberately not mapped. Weight 80 rather than 100 is the honest arithmetic of that: the permit sections are the operative axis of a larger act.', 'https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/BILLS-119hr3898eh/html/BILLS-119hr3898eh.htm')
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 3628 · energy_production w90 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 2 amends section 111(d) of the Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act
  --   of 1978 (16 U.S.C. 2621(d)) by adding standard (22), 'Ensuring electric
  --   reliability with reliable generation facilities', which each State
  --   regulated electric utility's regulatory authority must consider.
  --   energy_production's written scope names 'dispatchable-resource state
  --   policy' among the instruments that are IN, and this is the federal
  --   instrument that creates one. Weight 90 because the Act's own short title
  --   names affordability alongside reliability, a motive beyond the key.
  --
  --   DECLINED states_federal_power: Refused twice over. First on the engine:
  --   states_federal_power is one of the thirteen `_RD_NO_POLE` keys, so every
  --   read on it returns reason `no_pole` and a row here would add an act that
  --   can never be characterised. Second on the merits: a PURPA consideration
  --   standard is a direction about which generation a State should plan for,
  --   not a claim about whose rule governs — PURPA has assigned States
  --   consideration duties since 1978 and nobody's federalism position is
  --   revealed by standard (22).
  --
  --   DECLINED cost_living: The Act's short title names affordability and
  --   cost_living's published chip names utility prices, so the row was
  --   considered and refused. The operative text adds a reliability
  --   consideration standard for State regulators and sets no rate, no cap and
  --   no bill-relief mechanism; nothing in it lowers a utility bill except by an
  --   argument about what reliable generation costs, which is a forecast rather
  --   than an instrument. Runbook rule 4's principle applies to a short title as
  --   much as to a truncated purpose line: the name is not the instrument.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 4090 · lands_energy w100 PRIMARY yea_supports
  --   SEC. 4 (PRIORITY PROJECTS) is the operative section and it is a duty, not
  --   a study: within ten days of enactment and annually after, the Secretary
  --   must send Congress a list of every mining project on Federal land with a
  --   pending plan of operations or permit application, and within ten days of
  --   that list must identify each priority project the Secretary 'can
  --   immediately approve' and then 'take all necessary and appropriate action
  --   to approve or issue' it. SEC. 5 directs an assessment of mining potential
  --   on Federal land and SEC. 6 a regulatory review, but the Act does not stop
  --   at either. lands_energy's published chip is 'Expand domestic energy,
  --   mining & resource development' and a ten-day approval duty over federal
  --   mining permits is that, so weight 100.
  --
  --   DECLINED permitting_reform: Argued and refused. SEC. 4 does compress a
  --   permit timeline, which is why it was considered, but permitting_reform is
  --   about the review itself — its scope, its page and deadline limits, its
  --   lead agency and the window to sue. H.R. 4090 changes none of those; it
  --   directs a Secretary to approve a class of applications under the law as it
  --   stands. The distinction matters because the two keys read the same bill
  --   differently: one would say the member voted to shorten reviews generally,
  --   the other that they voted to open federal land to mining, and only the
  --   second is in the text.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: SEC. 6 is captioned REGULATORY REVIEW, and
  --   runbook rule 3 is exactly the rule that stops a caption from carrying a
  --   key. The review is of mining regulations in service of SEC. 4's approval
  --   duty.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

  -- H.R. 4690 · climate_action w100 PRIMARY yea_opposes
  --   SEC. 2 strikes subclauses (I) and (II) of section 305(a)(3)(D)(i) of the
  --   Energy Conservation and Production Act (42 U.S.C. 6834(a)(3)(D)(i)) — the
  --   fossil-fuel-use reduction targets for new and renovated Federal buildings
  --   — and then provides that subpart B of part 433 and subpart B of part 435
  --   of title 10, Code of Federal Regulations 'shall have no force or effect',
  --   with a transition clause implementing the statute 'as though the
  --   requirements of subclauses (I) and (II) had never taken effect'.
  --   Subsections (a)(2) and (c) further bar the green-building certification
  --   system from withholding certification 'solely based on direct or indirect
  --   consumption of fossil fuels'. climate_action's written scope names
  --   'Programmes, subsidies and rules that cut emissions, and their repeal' as
  --   what is IN, and its pole reads 'Against = it repealed, defunded or taxed
  --   one'. This is a repeal, so the row is yea_opposes and a yea is published
  --   as against the key. Weight 100: the repeal is the entire measure.
  --
  --   DECLINED energy_production: No conventional supply is expanded and no
  --   generating unit is kept online. The Act governs how Federal buildings are
  --   built. energy_production's scope note names emissions programmes as OUT
  --   and points at climate_action, which is where the row went.
  --
  --   DECLINED gov_regulation: Runbook rule 3. The Act voids two named CFR
  --   subparts, which is a rule repeal in service of a specific policy —
  --   building energy standards — and not a position on the rulebook as such.
  --   The polarity above already publishes the direction honestly.
  ON CONFLICT (measure_id, issue_key) DO NOTHING;

END $$;

-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- VERIFICATION — scoped to THIS WAVE'S eleven rolls and eleven measures.
-- Nothing below counts a session-wide or corpus-wide total. A guard that fails
-- because some other wave landed is a guard that gets deleted, so every count here
-- is taken over the named rolls and the named measures only.
-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DO $$
DECLARE
  n_measures integer; n_rolls integer; n_votes integer; n_orphan integer; n_issues integer;
  n_nonprimary integer; n_badpos integer; n_thin integer; n_tally integer; n_hr1069 integer;
  n_keys integer; n_newkeys integer;
  roll_ids integer[];
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_measures FROM vr_measures
   WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690');
  IF n_measures <> 11 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: expected 11 measures on file, found %', n_measures;
  END IF;

  SELECT array_agg(id) INTO roll_ids FROM vr_rollcalls WHERE chamber = 'house'
    AND (congress, session, roll_number) IN ((119,1,279), (119,1,277), (119,1,358), (119,1,356), (119,1,347), (119,1,342), (119,1,334), (119,1,330), (119,1,323), (119,2,55), (119,2,134));
  n_rolls := coalesce(array_length(roll_ids, 1), 0);
  IF n_rolls <> 11 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: expected 11 roll calls, found %', n_rolls;
  END IF;

  SELECT count(*) INTO n_votes FROM vr_member_votes WHERE rollcall_id = ANY(roll_ids);
  IF n_votes < 1284 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: expected at least 1284 member votes on these rolls, found %', n_votes;
  END IF;

  -- Fail-closed attribution, asserted rather than assumed. A politician_id on one of
  -- these rolls that is not in the roster means the generator guessed at somebody,
  -- which is the one failure mode surname matching would have introduced.
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_orphan FROM vr_member_votes v
   WHERE v.rollcall_id = ANY(roll_ids)
     AND v.politician_id NOT IN ('adam_smith', 'adrian_smith', 'aguilar', 'andy_harris', 'angie_craig', 'aoc', 'arrington', 'ayanna_pressley', 'bennie_thompson', 'bmoore', 'boebert', 'brendan_boyle', 'brian_babin', 'brian_mast', 'bruce_westerman', 'bryan_steil', 'chellie_pingree', 'chip_roy', 'clyburn', 'comer', 'crockett', 'dan_crenshaw', 'dan_goldman', 'debbie_dingell', 'delauro', 'delia_ramirez', 'diana_degette', 'dina_titus', 'don_bacon', 'don_davis', 'donalds', 'emmer', 'fitzpatrick', 'foxx', 'frank_lucas', 'french_hill', 'gabe_vasquez', 'garbarino', 'glenn_thompson', 'greg_landsman', 'guthrie', 'haley_stevens', 'jake_auchincloss', 'jan_schakowsky', 'jared_golden', 'jared_huffman', 'jason_smith', 'jayapal', 'jeffries', 'jim_himes', 'jim_jordan', 'jim_mcgovern', 'josh_brecheen', 'josh_gottheimer', 'julie_fedorchak', 'kclark', 'kennedy', 'kevin_hern', 'khanna', 'luna', 'maloy', 'mariannette_miller_meeks', 'marie_gluesenkamp_perez', 'massie', 'maxine_waters', 'maxwell_frost', 'mcclain', 'meeks', 'melanie_stansbury', 'michael_guest', 'mike_bost', 'mike_collins', 'mike_ezell', 'mike_flood', 'mike_johnson', 'mike_lawler', 'mike_simpson', 'mtg', 'nadler', 'nancy_mace', 'neal', 'neguse', 'omar', 'owens', 'pallone', 'raja_krishnamoorthi', 'raskin', 'rick_crawford', 'rick_larsen', 'rob_bresnahan', 'robert_garcia', 'roger_williams', 'russ_fulcher', 'ryan_mackenzie', 'sam_graves', 'sarah_mcbride', 'scalise', 'scott_perry', 'seth_moulton', 'stefanik', 'steny_hoyer', 'stephanie_bice', 'steve_womack', 'summer_lee', 'susie_lee', 'takano', 'ted_lieu', 'teresa_leger_fernandez', 'tlaib', 'tom_cole', 'tom_suozzi', 'torres', 'trent_kelly', 'troy_downing', 'walberg', 'zach_nunn', 'zoe_lofgren');
  IF n_orphan > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: % member vote(s) on these rolls carry a politician_id outside db/vr-member-map.json. Attribution here is fail-closed, so this is a bug, not a roster gap.', n_orphan;
  END IF;

  SELECT count(*) INTO n_badpos FROM vr_member_votes
   WHERE rollcall_id = ANY(roll_ids)
     AND position NOT IN ('yea','nay','present','not_voting');
  IF n_badpos > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: % member vote(s) carry a position outside the closed vocabulary.', n_badpos;
  END IF;

  -- The tally is the chamber's, not the roster's. If these ever match the attributed
  -- row count, somebody recomputed a total from the rows and the site is publishing a
  -- vote that did not happen.
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_tally FROM vr_rollcalls r
   WHERE r.id = ANY(roll_ids)
     AND (r.totals->>'yea')::int + (r.totals->>'nay')::int
         <= (SELECT count(*) FROM vr_member_votes v WHERE v.rollcall_id = r.id);
  IF n_tally > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: % roll(s) have a yea+nay pool no larger than their attributed row count. Totals must be the full chamber, never the roster subset.', n_tally;
  END IF;

  SELECT count(*) INTO n_issues FROM vr_measure_issues
   WHERE measure_id IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690'));
  IF n_issues <> 11 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: expected exactly 11 issue mappings on this wave''s measures, found %. A higher count means somebody added a row to feed coverage; the seed refuses 22 keys on these eleven measures in writing.', n_issues;
  END IF;

  -- Rule 30's wall, asserted. Every row this wave writes is primary on purpose.
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_nonprimary FROM vr_measure_issues
   WHERE measure_id IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690'))
     AND is_primary = false;
  IF n_nonprimary > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: % row(s) on this wave''s measures are non-primary. Rule 30: a fourth non-primary act on a key flips a member from thin-but-one-sided to not-about-this-issue, so this wave supplies only primaries.', n_nonprimary;
  END IF;

  -- No new vocabulary. Every key used here must already appear on a measure outside
  -- this wave, which is the strongest available assertion that nothing was invented.
  SELECT count(DISTINCT issue_key) INTO n_keys FROM vr_measure_issues
   WHERE measure_id IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690'));
  IF n_keys <> 4 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: this wave writes % distinct keys, expected 4 (climate_action, energy_production, lands_energy, permitting_reform).', n_keys;
  END IF;
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_newkeys FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT i.issue_key FROM vr_measure_issues i
     WHERE i.measure_id IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690'))
       AND NOT EXISTS (
         SELECT 1 FROM vr_measure_issues j WHERE j.issue_key = i.issue_key
          AND j.measure_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE measure_type = 'bill' AND congress = 119 AND chamber = 'house' AND number IN ('H.R. 1047', 'H.R. 3062', 'H.R. 1366', 'H.R. 4776', 'H.R. 3616', 'H.R. 3632', 'H.R. 3668', 'H.R. 3898', 'H.R. 3628', 'H.R. 4090', 'H.R. 4690')))
  ) t;
  IF n_newkeys > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: % key(s) exist only on this wave''s measures. This wave adds NO vocabulary — the District of Columbia key it considered is refused in writing under vocab rule 5 — so a key with no prior instrument means one was invented here.', n_newkeys;
  END IF;

  -- The standing refusal, re-asserted where it can actually be checked.
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_hr1069 FROM vr_measure_issues
   WHERE measure_id IN (SELECT id FROM vr_measures WHERE congress = 119 AND number IN ('H.R. 1069','H.R. 973'));
  IF n_hr1069 > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: H.R. 1069 / H.R. 973 now carry % issue row(s). Both are deliberately unmapped — F1 argued and refused the public_schools row, and rule 3 refuses gov_regulation on H.R. 973. No vocab decision in F6 reopens either.', n_hr1069;
  END IF;

  -- The conversion this wave exists for, asserted on the eight named members. The
  -- member floor counts MAPPED records, so this counts mapped ones.
  SELECT min(c) INTO n_thin FROM (
    SELECT count(DISTINCT r.measure_id) AS c
      FROM vr_member_votes v
      JOIN vr_rollcalls r ON r.id = v.rollcall_id
     WHERE v.politician_id IN ('adrian_smith','dina_titus','gabe_vasquez','melanie_stansbury','russ_fulcher','susie_lee','teresa_leger_fernandez','zach_nunn')
       AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM vr_measure_issues i WHERE i.measure_id = r.measure_id)
     GROUP BY v.politician_id
  ) t;
  IF n_thin IS NULL OR n_thin < 12 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Federal wave F6: the thinnest of the eight named House members holds % mapped act(s), below the member floor of 12. The wave''s whole purpose is that conversion; _PDX_RD_MEMBER_FLOOR is never lowered to meet it.', n_thin;
  END IF;

  RAISE NOTICE 'Federal wave F6 verified: % measures, % rolls, % member votes, % issue rows, thinnest of the eight named members at % mapped acts.', n_measures, n_rolls, n_votes, n_issues, n_thin;
END $$;
